    "evidence_text": "However, evaluation for this task remains a challenging problem.\n\nWe demonstrate the potential of our evaluation framework by evaluating existing image captioning models on a wide ranging set of synthetic datasets that we construct for diagnostic evaluation.\n\nWe empirically show how the GTD evaluation framework, in combination with diagnostic datasets, can provide insights into model capabilities and limitations to supplement standard evaluations.\n\nMany existing captioning models , , ,  follow the typical encoder-decoder framework where\n\nEvaluating candidate captions for human preference is slow and laborious.\n\nThese n-gram-based metrics evaluate captioning performance based on surface similarity between a candidate caption and reference statements.\n\nConsider the example image and captions from the ShapeWorld framework  shown in Figure\n\nFIGREF1.\n\nPractical evaluation of GTD is currently only possible on synthetic data.\n\nWe construct a range of datasets designed for image captioning evaluation.\n\nWe illustrate the evaluation of specific image captioning models on ShapeWorldICE.\n\nWe\n\nSPICE  is a more recent metric specifically designed for image captioning.\n\nIn contrast, our truthfulness metric directly evaluates a candidate caption against a model of the actual visual content.\n\nMany researchers have pointed out problems with existing reference-based metrics including\n\nstrengths and limitations of existing models.\n\nIn this work, we develop the evaluation datasets within the ShapeWorld framework.\n\nWe use ShapeWorld to generate training and evaluation data for two major reasons.\n\nDuring data generation, ShapeWorld randomly samples a world model from a set of available entities and attributes.\n\nThe generated world model is then used to realize a corresponding instance consisting of image and caption.\n\nspecific metric.\n\nGTD Evaluation Framework ::: Truthfulness\tThe second aspect we investigate is truthfulness,\n\nEach converted DMRS is a logical semantic graph representation corresponding to the caption.\n\nTable TABREF4 gives an overview for different ShapeWorldICE datasets we use in this paper.\n\nExistential descriptions examine whether a certain object is present in an image.\n\nQuantification descriptions involve count-based and ratio-based statements, with an explicit focus on inspecting models for their counting ability.\n\nAll the training and test captions sampled in this work are in English.\n\nAt test time, only the test images are available to the evaluated models.\n\nUnderlying world models are kept from the models and are used for later GTD evaluation.\n\nCorrelation between the BLEU/SPICE scores and the ground truth.\n\nCaptions generated on Spatial-MultiShapes attain a\n\nsee that while the model performs rather well on Existential datasets, it exhibits a worse performance on Spatial data.\n\nCounting has long been considered to be a challenging task in multimodal reasoning , .\n\nCaption diversity benefits from",
